comments.message:
I’m referring to the following section in pidcontroller.cpp:

// Under the hysteresis bounds, initialize pid
else if (input < (setpt - info->negativeHysteresis))
{
    lastInput = setpt;
    info->integral = 0;
    output = 0;
}


In a traditional PID design, the controller normally continues integrating across the entire operating range — it doesn’t reinitialize or reset the integral term like this.
This behavior was probably introduced to prevent integral wind-up when the input stays far below the setpoint.
However, since the current implementation already provides ILimitMin and ILimitMax parameters, proper configuration of these limits should be sufficient to prevent wind-up without needing to reset the PID state.
